## Schema

```sql
-- migration 000194 (up)
ALTER TABLE alert_rules
    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS max_fires_per_resolution INTEGER NULL
    CHECK (max_fires_per_resolution IS NULL OR max_fires_per_resolution > 0);

COMMENT ON COLUMN alert_rules.max_fires_per_resolution IS
    'For repeat-mode rules: stop firing after N fires until the condition
     resolves (falling edge clears the counter). NULL = unlimited (legacy
     behaviour). Once-mode rules ignore this column — the latch already
     caps them at 1 per resolution.';
```

Counter side already exists from slice 0002:
`alert_rule_state.fire_count_since_reset`, incremented by
`alertRuleStateMarkFiredSQL`, reset by `alertRuleStateClearLatchSQL`.

---

## Engine logic

After the cooldown check passes and before `MarkFired`:

```go
if rule.TriggerMode != "once" &&
   rule.MaxFiresPerResolution != nil &&
   fireCount >= *rule.MaxFiresPerResolution {
    metrics.AlertRulesMaxFiresCapHit.Inc()
    return EvalResult{}
}
```

Once-mode rules bypass the cap because the existing latch already
suppresses them after fire #1. Adding the cap on top would just be
redundant — the test
`TestEvaluate_MaxFiresCap_OnceMode_NoEffect` proves the latch still
wins (fire count stays at exactly 1 even with `cap=3`).

The capped 4th evaluation does NOT call `MarkFired`. This was a
deliberate decision (and is asserted by
`TestEvaluate_MaxFiresCap_SuppressesAfterCap`): silently bumping the
DB counter past the cap on a suppressed eval would leave the next pod
restart re-suppressing on the wrong baseline.

